# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository stores text with LF, which is what every blob already contains.
# Without an explicit rule, Git for Windows applies `core.autocrlf=true` from the
# system config and expects CRLF in the working tree. That expectation produced a
# permanent "modified" status on 35 files whose bytes were in fact identical to
# the index, which made `git status` useless for spotting real changes.
#
# `eol=lf` states the intent once, so the working tree, the index and the blobs
# all agree. Text files are detected automatically; binary files are left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Git LFS. Must come after the general rule so that it wins for these paths.
marketing/assets/fonts/*.ttf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Detached signatures are binary artifacts, not text: the verifier hashes their
# exact bytes. Under `text=auto` Git would strip CRLF from a signature that
# happens to contain one, silently invalidating it. Measured 2026-09-28: all
# nine evidence signatures are currently NUL-containing and survive untouched,
# but that is luck, not policy.
tests/fixtures/production_evidence/*.sig binary
